              "type": "application",
              "title": "Widen Your Own Art Lens",
              "content": "You don't need a museum membership in a major city to start seeing contemporary art more globally. Here's how to apply what you just learned:\n\n1. **Next time you read an art \"best of\" list**, notice how many countries and continents are represented — and how many aren't\n\n2. **Look up your nearest museum's non-Western contemporary holdings.** Most major museums now have digital collection databases you can search from home\n\n3. **Follow one artist from this lesson** — Kusama, El Anatsui, Ai Weiwei, or research Gutai's remaining members — and see what they're making right now, not just historically\n\n4. **When you encounter unfamiliar art traditions** (like kente-cloth-inspired sculpture or Gutai performance), research the specific cultural and historical context before judging the work by Western-canon standards alone\n\n5. **Notice institutional curation.** When you see a major exhibition, ask who curated it and what perspective they bring — that shapes everything you're seeing",
